# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.pyo
*.pyd
.Python
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Data
# The live working database stays out: it is in WAL mode, churns on every
# evaluation, and cannot be copied safely while anything is writing to it.
data/*.db
# WAL mode leaves these two beside the DB; they are transient and machine-local.
data/*.db-wal
data/*.db-shm

# The vacuumed snapshot IS committed, so a second machine gets the whole corpus
# without re-importing from OpenAlex or re-running hours of local inference.
# Affordable because VACUUM INTO lays pages out deterministically, so git deltas
# successive versions to almost nothing: 4.4 MB for the first commit, then about
# 20 KB per 300 new verdicts - roughly 340 KB for an entire re-run. Measured, not
# assumed. Refresh it with `python backend/snapshot_db.py`.
!data/claims-snapshot.db
data/*.json

# Pre-built frontend JSON IS committed so Vercel can serve it statically
# frontend/public/data/

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb_meta

# Node / React
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/.pnp
frontend/.pnp.js
frontend/build/
frontend/.cache/

# Env
.env
.env.local
.env.*.local
*.env

# Editor
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*

# Labelling happens in Numbers, but gold_set.csv is the canonical artifact and
# the only thing the harness reads. `bakeoff.py` exports the spreadsheet over
# the CSV whenever the spreadsheet is newer, so committing both would mean a
# 300 KB binary churning alongside the diff that actually shows what changed.
gold/*.numbers

# Bake-off run caches. A working artifact, like the live database: cheap to
# regenerate (minutes, not the hours a full pass costs) and usually partial.
# What survives a run is the SCORE, which is recorded in DECISIONS.md.
gold/runs/
